.TH ROTBYTE 1 "2026-07-10" "rotbyte 1.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rotbyte \- detect bit rot by tracking BLAKE2b checksums
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rotbyte
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rotbyte
computes BLAKE2b-512 checksums for every file in a directory tree and
stores them in a local SQLite database. On subsequent runs it detects
silent data corruption (bit rot) by comparing stored checksums against
freshly computed ones.
.PP
By default, only files whose size or modification time have changed are
re-hashed. Use
.B \-\-check
to force a full re-verify of every file, which is the only way to catch
true bit rot (corruption without metadata change).
.PP
The database is created automatically as
.BI . dirname _rotbyte.db
inside the target directory on first run.
.PP
Sending SIGINT (Ctrl\-C) once causes the current batch to finish, saves
progress, and exits with code 3. Sending SIGINT a second time while a
graceful shutdown is in progress aborts immediately.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Force re-hash of every file regardless of modification time.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Print an integrity status report and exit. The report includes: a count
of tracked files grouped by status (OK, NEW, FAILED, MISSING); full
details for any FAILED files including expected and actual checksums; and
a list of files not verified in 90 or more days.
.TP
.BI \-\-accept " FILE"
Accept a single file's current state as correct. If the file is MISSING,
its record is removed. If FAILED, it is re-hashed and the new checksum
becomes the known-good baseline.
.TP
.B \-\-accept\-all
Accept all current state as the new baseline. Clears all MISSING records
and re-hashes all FAILED files.
.TP
.B \-\-import
Import existing
.I .b2sum
and
.I .b2
hash files into the database. Verifies each hash matches the file on disk
before importing, then deletes the hash file.
Files already tracked in the database with a status other than MISSING or
FAILED are skipped and reported; they are not re-imported.
MISSING or FAILED records are accepted and updated if the hash matches.
.TP
.BI \-\-workers " N"
Number of parallel hashing workers. Defaults to the CPU count.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress normal output; only print problems. Suitable for cron jobs.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-missing
Do not check for files that have been removed from disk.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-hidden
Include hidden files and directories (names starting with \fB.\fR),
which are excluded by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATH" \fR...
Exclude one or more directories. Paths may be relative to the target
directory or absolute.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Use a custom database path instead of the default location inside the
target directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-export " FILE"
Export a plain-text manifest of all tracked file checksums to FILE.
Format is b2sum-compatible: one line per file with the BLAKE2b-512
baseline checksum followed by the file path. Missing files are excluded.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output results as a JSON object instead of human-readable text. Suppresses
progress bars and spinners. Suitable for scripts and monitoring pipelines.
.PP
The JSON object contains the following keys:
.RS
.TP
.B status
\fIcomplete\fR or \fIinterrupted\fR.
.TP
.B directory
Absolute path of the scanned directory.
.TP
.B duration_seconds
Elapsed wall time as a floating-point number.
.TP
.B bytes_hashed
Total bytes read during hashing.
.TP
.BR new ", " updated ", " verified_ok ", " failed ", " missing ", " skipped ", " errors ", " likely_moves
Integer counters for each outcome category.
.TP
.B failed_files
Array of file paths for all FAILED files.
Present only when
.B failed
is greater than zero.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-budget " DURATION"
Time budget for
.B \-\-check
scans (e.g.
.IR 2h ", " 30m ", " 1h30m ).
Files are verified stalest-first so each run covers a different portion
of the database. Over successive runs, everything gets checked.
Without
.BR \-\-track ,
requires
.B \-\-check
or
.BR \-\-due .
When used with
.BR \-\-track ,
also requires
.BR \-\-full\-at .
.TP
.BI \-\-due " DURATION"
Only re-verify files whose last verification is older than the given
duration. Accepts values in the form
.I Nd
where N is a number of days (e.g.
.IR 30d ", " 7d ", " 90d ).
Implies
.BR \-\-check .
Combines naturally with
.B \-\-budget
to create time-limited incremental scans.
.TP
.B \-\-track
Install platform-native scheduled scans. On macOS, writes launchd plists
to
.IR ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ .
On Linux, writes systemd user timer and service units to
.IR ~/.config/systemd/user/ .
Combine with
.BR \-\-every ", " \-\-full\-at ", and " \-\-budget
to configure the schedule.
.TP
.B \-\-track\-setup
Interactive setup wizard for
.BR \-\-track .
Prompts in order for: target directory, quick scan frequency
.RB ( \-\-every ),
full scan clock times
.RB ( \-\-full\-at ),
time budget per full scan
.RB ( \-\-budget ),
due threshold
.RB ( \-\-due ),
and whether to enable email notifications
.RB ( \-\-notify " " email ).
Each value is validated with the same parser used by the corresponding flag
and the user is re-prompted on invalid input. Before installing, prints the
equivalent
.B \-\-track
command line so the user can learn the flag form for future use.
Email notifications require prior configuration via
.BR \-\-notify\-setup ;
if the config is absent, the wizard continues without email rather than
installing a broken schedule.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Show the status of all installed scheduled scans. For each tracked
directory, displays: the quick and full scan schedule and whether each
timer is active; the next scheduled full-scan time with a countdown (for
active calendar schedules); any extra flags baked into the schedule (such as
.BR \-\-budget ", " \-\-due ", " \-\-notify );
the finish time of the most recent run converted to local time (a run that
started but never completed is shown as in progress or interrupted); and a
count of files by health status (OK, NEW, FAILED, MISSING) with the paths of
up to five FAILED files. If no scheduled scans are found, suggests the
.B \-\-track
command.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
Re-point every installed scheduled scan at the interpreter and script path
that this rotbyte resolves to today, then reload it. Fixes schedules broken
by a package upgrade that deleted the old paths (which otherwise fail
silently at launch and show as
.B BROKEN
in
.BR \-\-status ).
The rewrite happens in place and preserves every flag and the target
directory; schedules already on the current path are left untouched. Safe to
run at any time — in particular after every
.B brew upgrade
of rotbyte or Python.
.TP
.BI \-\-untrack " [PATH]"
Remove the scheduled rotbyte runs that were installed for the given
directory. The path is canonicalised the same way
.B \-\-track
canonicalises it at install time (via
.BR realpath (3)),
so the same directory always maps to the same scheduled units. If
.I PATH
is omitted the current working directory is used.
.IP
On macOS the launchd agent is bootout-ed (or unloaded on older releases)
and the plist file under
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
is deleted. On Linux the systemd user timer and service are disabled,
their unit files removed from
.IR ~/.config/systemd/user/ ,
and
.B systemctl --user daemon-reload
is run. On Windows the corresponding Task Scheduler tasks under
.I \\rotbyte\\
are deleted with
.BR schtasks.exe .
.IP
The checksum database itself is not touched. If no scheduled run is
installed for the target directory, a friendly notice is printed and
rotbyte exits 0 \(em this is not an error.
.TP
.B \-\-untrack\-all
Remove every scheduled rotbyte run on this machine across all tracked
directories. Discovers installed units the same way
.B \-\-status
does and removes each one. Prints the list of removed units and a count.
Exits 0 even when nothing was found.
.IP
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-untrack .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-logs
Clear rotbyte's scheduler logs. On macOS the live log of each still-installed
scan is truncated in place (launchd holds the log file open between runs, so
deleting it would leak disk until the next reload), while rotated generations
(\fI.log.1\fR, \fI.log.2\fR) and orphaned logs left behind by a past
.B \-\-untrack
or
.B \-\-repair
are deleted. Only macOS keeps rotbyte-owned log files; on Linux the
scheduled-scan output lives in the systemd journal and on Windows in Task
Scheduler's history, so on those platforms
.B \-\-clear\-logs
prints where to look
.RB ( journalctl " / Task Scheduler)"
instead of deleting anything. The checksum databases are never touched. Exits 0
even when there is nothing to clear.
.TP
.BI \-\-every " INTERVAL"
Quick scan frequency when using
.BR \-\-track .
Accepts durations like
.IR 30m ", " 2h ", " 2h30m .
Default:
.IR 60m .
Requires
.BR \-\-track .
.TP
.BI \-\-full\-at " TIME" \fR...
One or more daily clock times for full
.B \-\-check
scans when using
.BR \-\-track .
Accepts clock times like
.IR 2h " (2:00 AM), " 14h30m " (2:30 PM)."
Requires
.BR \-\-track .
.TP
.BI \-\-notify " BACKEND"
Send a notification on scan completion.
Currently supported backends:
.IR email .
Requires prior configuration via
.BR \-\-notify\-setup .
.PP
When used with
.BR \-\-check ,
a notification is always sent. The subject line reflects one of four states:
.RS
.TP
.B pass
Full re-verify completed with no problems and no read errors.
Subject:
.RI \(lq "rotbyte \(u2713 /path \(em all files OK" \(rq.
.TP
.B warning
Full re-verify completed, checksums clean, but one or more files could not
be read (hardware error, permissions, or file in use).
Subject:
.RI \(lq "rotbyte \(u26a0 /path \(em N read errors" \(rq.
.TP
.B fail
Bit rot or missing files detected.
Subject:
.RI \(lq "rotbyte \(u2717 /path \(em N failed, N missing" \(rq.
.TP
.B interrupted
Scan was cut short by SIGINT or SIGTERM before all files were verified.
Subject:
.RI \(lq "rotbyte \(u26a0 /path \(em scan interrupted" \(rq.
.RE
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-check
(quick scan), a notification is only sent in the fail state.
.PP
Can be used standalone or with
.BR \-\-track ,
which persists the flag into the scheduled commands.
.TP
.BI \-\-notify\-setup " BACKEND"
Interactive setup for notifications. Prompts in order: username,
password, SMTP host, SMTP port, recipient address (Send alerts to),
and sender address (Send alerts from). All fields default to the username
except Send alerts from, which also defaults to the username but can be
set to an alias (useful for iCloud alias addresses). Stores credentials in
.IR ~/.config/rotbyte/notify.conf
(Linux) or
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/rotbyte/notify.conf"
(macOS) with 0600 permissions, and sends a test email to confirm
the configuration works.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All files verified OK.
.TP
.B 1
Missing files detected.
.TP
.B 2
Bit rot detected (checksum mismatch).
.TP
.B 3
Run was interrupted (safe to re-run).
.TP
.B 4
Database integrity check failed (restore from backup).
.TP
.B 5
Database locked by another rotbyte process (retry later).
.TP
.B 6
I/O error (target directory unreachable, permission denied).
.TP
.B 7
Internal error (worker pool died, unexpected exception).
.SH FILE STATUSES
.TP
.B OK
Checksum verified; file is intact.
.TP
.B NEW
File seen for the first time.
.TP
.B FAILED
Bit rot detected \(em hash changed without any metadata change.
.TP
.B MISSING
File was previously tracked but no longer exists on disk.
.SH EXAMPLES
Index all files in the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte
.RE
.PP
Full re-verify of an external drive:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-check /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Weekly cron job (quiet mode, log errors):
.PP
.RS
.B 0 2 * * 0 rotbyte \-\-check \-q /data >> /var/log/rotbyte.log 2>&1
.RE
.PP
Accept a restored file:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-accept restored_file.mkv
.RE
.PP
Full re-verify with a two-hour time budget:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-check \-\-budget 2h /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Install scheduled scans (quick every 30 min, full re-verify daily at 2 AM with budget):
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-track \-\-every 30m \-\-full\-at 2h \-\-budget 2h /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Interactive setup wizard for scheduled scans:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-track\-setup /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Set up email notifications:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-notify\-setup email
.RE
.PP
Full re-verify with email health report:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-check \-\-notify email /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Scheduled scans with email alerts:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-track \-\-every 1h \-\-full\-at 2h \-\-notify email /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Remove scheduled scans for a directory:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-untrack /Volumes/Media
.RE
.PP
Remove all scheduled scans:
.PP
.RS
.B rotbyte \-\-untrack\-all
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI . dirname _rotbyte.db
SQLite database storing checksums and metadata, created inside the
target directory.
.TP
.BI . dirname _rotbyte.db.lock
Lock file preventing concurrent runs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
On Linux, overrides the base directory used to locate the notification
config. Default:
.IR ~/.config .
The config is read from
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /rotbyte/notify.conf .
.SH NOTES
.B rotbyte
uses BLAKE2b-512 for hashing, which is faster than SHA-256 while
providing equivalent collision resistance. Hashing is parallelized
across multiple processes for maximum throughput on multi-core systems.
.PP
Symlinks are not followed to prevent infinite loops.
.SH CAVEATS
.PP
.B rotbyte
is POSIX-only and requires
.BR fcntl (2)
for file locking. It does not run on Windows.
.PP
On macOS, scheduled scans installed with
.B \-\-track
require Full Disk Access to be granted to the Python interpreter when
scanning TCC-protected directories (Desktop, Documents, Downloads,
external volumes). See
.I docs/macOS\ Permissions.md
in the source distribution for the setup procedure.
.PP
If a
.B rotbyte
process is killed with SIGKILL (or otherwise hard-killed), the lock file
.BI ( .dirname _rotbyte.db.lock )
will not be removed automatically. Delete it manually before re-running.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Python 3.9 or later. macOS or Linux (uses
.BR fcntl (2)
for file locking).
.SH AUTHORS
Jamey Wicklund
.UR https://github.com/TheBluWiz
.UE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR b2sum (1),
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1)
